\h Esther
\toc1 Esther
\toc2 Esther
\toc3 Est.
\mt1 Esther
\is1 Introduction
\ip This account tells about the life of a young Jewish woman named Esther. These events happened back when King Ahasuerus (more widely known as King Xerxes) ruled over the kingdom of Persia. After Queen Vashti was banished for refusing to dance in front the king's guests at a drinking party, Esther was chosen as the new queen without revealing her Jewish background. But there was a high official called Haman who was offended by Esther's Jewish guardian and in his anger, determined to annihilate all of the Jews from the kingdom. This account reveals how Ether's courage and her love for her Jewish people leads her to help them and eventually helps to save their lives.
\ip The account also reveals the source of a Jewish feast named ‘Purim’, which to this day is a time when Jewish people celebrate how God saved them from their enemy, Haman.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Esther becomes Queen of Persia \ior 1:1–2:23\ior*
\io1 Haman's threat to the Jews \ior 3:1–5:14\ior*
\io1 Haman's execution \ior 6:1–7:10\ior*
\io1 The Jewish people destroy their enemies \ior 8:1–10:3\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Empress Vashti refuses Emperor Ahasuerus
\rem /s1 King Ahasuerus's banquet; Queen Vashti Deposed; Queen Vashti Defies King Xerxes; King Ahasuerus Deposes Queen Vashti; The King's Banquet
\p
\v 1 The following events happened during the reign¦228506 of \add Emperor\add* Ahasuerus¦228503.\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Ezr 4:6.\x* (\add Also known as King Xerxes,\add* he¦228501 ruled the 127 provinces¦228514 \add of Persia extending\add* from¦228507 India¦228507 to Ethiopia¦228510.)
\v 2 In¦228521 those¦228517 days¦228516, he ruled his¦228524 empire from Shushan¦228526,\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Known as ‘Susa’ in many 20th century translations, even though earlier English translations often got it correct.\f* city.
\p
\v 3 In the third year¦228529 of his reign¦228531, he¦228532 hosted a celebration for all¦228534 of his officials and for every important person who worked for him¦228545, the wealthy landowners, the officials of the provinces¦228544, as well as the officers who served in the army¦228538 of the \add combined empire\add* of Persia¦228540 and Media¦228541. He was present¦228545 in person
\v 4 \add to host\add* the six-month celebration which, displayed¦228547 the incredible wealth and prestige of his empire and his personal wealth and power.
\p
\v 5 At¦228564 the end of that time, the emperor¦228569 hosted \add another\add* celebration, but¦228564 this time for all¦228570 the people¦228572 in the royal fort in Shushan¦228574, from the nobles to the least prestigious. He held this celebration over an entire week in the enclosed garden¦228584 of the royal palace¦228585.
\v 6 There were white and blue curtains hanging from white and purple¦228596 cords¦228593 attached to silver rings on marble pillars¦228601. The guests reclined on gold¦228605 and silver couches¦228603 set on a mosaic floor made of red and white marble, mother-of-pearl¦228612 and other expensive stones¦228613.
\v 7 Drinks¦228615 were¦228620 served¦228615 in¦228616 golden¦228617 goblets, and¦228621 each one was¦228623 a unique design. The king's¦228625 wine¦228621 seemed limitless as he displayed his generosity,
\v 8 and¦228646 it was served according¦228628 to his rule: there¦228631 was no compulsion to drink¦228627, but¦228627 his servants were to give each guest however much they wanted.
\p
\v 9 Meanwhile¦228658, Queen¦228650 Vashti¦228649 was hosting a celebration for the women¦228653 \add in a different part\add* of the royal¦228655 palace.
\rem /s1 Queen Vashti Deposed
\p
\v 10 On the last day¦228661 of the celebrations, when the emperor was pleasantly tipsy, he ordered seven¦228676 of his chief servants (Mehuman¦228669, Biztha¦228670, Harbona¦228671, Bigtha¦228672, Abagtha¦228673, Zethar¦228674, and Karkas)
\v 11 to bring¦228685 Queen¦228689 Vashti¦228688 to him, wearing her¦228699 royal¦228693 crown¦228692, in¦228692 order¦228694 to show¦228694 the¦228695 officials¦228696 and¦228696 other people¦228695 how good-looking¦228699 she was, because¦228700 she¦228704 was very beautiful¦228699.
\v 12 The¦228707 servants told her Ahasuerus's command¦228710, but¦228706 Queen¦228707 Vashti¦228708 refused¦228706 to come¦228709. When¦228706 they reported back to Ahasuerus, he¦228715 was¦228715 furious¦228715 and¦228706 \add sat there\add* raging¦228718 \add for a while\add*.
\p
\v 13-14 Eventually¦228724 he¦228722 discussed it¦228722 with his close¦228739 advisors who¦228725 knew¦228725 the law¦228736 and could make good decisions. (These seven¦228749 officials; Karshena, Shethar¦228743, Admatha¦228744, Tarshish¦228745, Meres¦228746, Marsena¦228747 and Memukan; were from \add various places.\add* They advised the emperor¦228756 personally, \add so were\add* the most powerful officials in the empire.)
\v 15 ‘According¦228761 to \add our\add* law¦228761,’ \add he asked,\add* ‘what¦228762 should be done with¦228765 Empress¦228773 Vashti¦228766, since she¦228772 refused to obey my command¦228775 when the servants passed it on¦228765 to her?’
\p
\v 16 Memukan replied¦228782, \add addressing\add* the whole¦228799 group, ‘Empress Vashti¦228794 has done wrong, and not¦228788 only against the emperor. She¦228793 has also done wrong against all¦228799 the officials¦228801 and people¦228806 groups of the empire.
\v 17 \add Soon\add* women all over the empire will hear about¦228817 what the empress¦228819 did and¦228838 they'll¦228828 say¦228828, “Emperor¦228829 Ahasuerus¦228830 commanded his¦228837 servants to bring¦228832 Empress Vashti¦228835 to him¦228837, but¦228838 she¦228840 didn't¦228838 go¦228816!” Then women will stop respecting their¦228826 husbands¦228826.
\v 18 Even today, the important wives in the empire will hear¦228851 what the empress¦228855 did and start doing the same to¦228856 their husbands; it will result in contempt¦228860 on her part and anger¦228861 on his.
\v 19 If it pleases you \add to do so\add*, \add your majesty\add*, you should personally issue a royal decree and have it added to the laws¦228875 of Persia¦228876 and Media¦228878 which cannot be repealed. This decree should say that Vashti¦228885 can never again come¦228884 into your presence. Then you should choose a different woman¦228892 for the royal position¦228889 – someone who's better¦228893 than¦228894 her¦228889.
\v 20 Have your¦228905 decree¦228897 proclaimed¦228896 throughout your empire, even though it's so huge¦228906, and¦228896 then¦228896 all women¦228910, whatever their status or position, will¦228911 respect¦228912 their husbands¦228913.’
\p
\v 21 Memukan's¦228927 advice seemed like a good¦228919 idea to¦228926 the¦228926 emperor and¦228919 his officials¦228923, so that's what they did.
\v 22 They wrote¦228941 letters to every¦228948 province¦228935 using its own alphabet and to each¦228933 people¦228944 group in its own language. The letters said that men¦228950 should be the masters over their wives and children, and that a husband should be able to give orders to his wife in his own language \add and that she should understand and obey him\add*.
\c 2
\s1 Beautiful Esther enters the king's harem
\rem /s1 Esther becomes queen; Esther Becomes Queen; Esther Made Queen
\p
\v 1 Some time later when King¦228963 Ahasuerus¦228964 no longer felt so angry, he \add started to miss\add* Vashti¦228968. But¦228969 when she'd disobeyed his command, he had made a decree \add that she could never come into his presence again\add*.
\v 2 So¦228979 some of the young men¦228980 who served¦228983 the king¦228982 suggested to him¦228983, “\add Your majesty, you should get a new wife for yourself.\add* You could tell your servants to look for young virgins¦228987 who are very beautiful.
\v 3 You could assign officers in each¦228994 province¦228996 of your empire to bring every¦229001 virgin¦229005 who is very beautiful here to your capital city of Shushan¦229010. They could stay in the harem for virgins under the custody of Hegai¦229019, the castrated royal guardian who takes care of the young women¦229023 who live there and¦228991 he could arrange for them to receive beauty treatments¦229025.
\v 4 Then¦229027 you could decide which young¦229027 woman¦229027 you like best and¦229027 then¦229027 make¦229039 her queen instead of Vashti¦229034.”
\p The king liked what they suggested¦229036, so¦229040 he¦229039 did it¦229039.
\p
\v 5 At that time, there was a Jewish man¦229043 named Mordekai¦229049 living in¦229046 the capital city of Shushan¦229046. (He¦229045 was from the tribe of Benjamin: his father was Jair¦229051, his grandfather was Shimei¦229054, and¦229048 his great-grandfather was Kish¦229057
\v 6 who'd been¦229062 taken¦229062 away from¦229063 Jerusalem¦229063 and led to Babylon when King¦229071 Nebuchadnezzer¦229076 of Babylon had¦229068 exiled¦229066 Jeconiah¦229070, the¦229071 king¦229071 of Judah¦229073, along with many of his people.)\x + \xo 2:6: \xt 2Ki 24:10-16; 2Ch 36:10.\x*
\v 7 Now Mordekai was taking¦229104 care of his cousin, who was an orphan. When¦229080 her father¦229093 and mother¦229094 had died, Mordekai had adopted¦229104 her as his own daughter¦229087. Her¦229102 \add Hebrew\add* name was Hadassah¦229084, and her \add Persian\add* name was Esther. Esther was now a young¦229095 woman¦229095 and she was exceptionally attractive.
\p
\v 8 So¦229109 when¦229110 the king's¦229113 command and decree became known, many young¦229116 women were being brought into¦229122 the capital city of Shushan¦229120 and placed under Hegai's¦229125 charge, and this is what happened to Esther¦229127. (Hegai was the overseer of the women.)
\v 9 Hegai was¦229139 very impressed with¦229141 Esther, and he¦229145 treated her favourably¦229143. He quickly¦229145 arranged for Esther to receive her beauty¦229148 treatments and her allotment of food, as well as choosing¦229157 seven¦229155 female servants¦229156 from the king's¦229162 palace¦229161 and assigning¦229158 them to be her personal attendants. He also moved¦229163 Esther and her attendants to the best rooms in¦229141 the harem for virgins.
\v 10 (Esther¦229174 hadn't revealed her family ties or her Jewishness to anyone, because¦229181 Mordekai¦229182 had¦229183 instructed¦229183 her not¦229186 to.)
\p
\v 11 Every day Mordekai¦229194 would walk¦229195 around in¦229209 front¦229196 of the courtyard¦229197 of the women's¦229200 harem to¦229201 find out \add from others\add* how Esther¦229205 was doing¦229208 and¦229190 how she was being treated.
\p
\v 12 Each young¦229213 woman¦229213 would get twelve months of beauty¦229233 treatment¦229233 according¦229224 to the \add Persian\add* law for women—there would be six months of applying oil¦229236 of myrrh¦229237, then¦229211 six months of perfumes¦229240 and¦229211 other lotions \add designed for\add* women. Then¦229211 their¦229233 turn¦229212 would come, one at¦229211 a time¦229232, to be taken to King¦229219 Ahasuerus¦229220.
\v 13 The¦229250 young¦229245 woman¦229245 was allowed to take¦229257 whatever she wanted from¦229259 the harem¦229259 when she went to the king's¦229264 house¦229263.
\v 14 She'd be taken across in the evening¦229266, and in the morning¦229270 she'd be led out to the other harem which was for the concubines¦229285. (That harem was overseen by Shaashgaz¦229281, another castrated royal guardian.) The women¦229276 taken there wouldn't go again¦229289 to the king's house¦229275 unless he¦229296 had especially liked them and summoned¦229299 her¦229297 back by name¦229300.
\s1 Esther becomes queen
\p
\v 15 Now Esther was the daughter of Mordekai's¦229310 uncle¦229309, Abihail¦229308, from whom¦229311 Mordekai had adopted her as his own daughter. When¦229302 it was her turn¦229303 to go¦229316 to the king, she¦229321 didn't¦229320 ask for anything¦229322 other than what Hegai¦229329, the king's¦229332 trusted official in¦229302 charge of the harem, had advised, and¦229302 everyone¦229333 who saw¦229342 Esther liked her.
\v 16 So¦229344 Esther¦229345 was¦229344 taken¦229344 to¦229346 King¦229348 Ahasuerus¦229349 at¦229354 his royal palace in January¦229359, in the seventh¦229362 year¦229360 of¦229358 his reign¦229363,
\v 17 and the king¦229366 loved¦229365 Esther¦229369 more than any of the other women. He treated her more kindly and more favourably¦229375 than any of the other young women \add who had become his concubines\add*. He put a royal¦229384 crown¦229382 on¦229385 her head¦229385 and made¦229386 her the \add new\add* queen¦229386 to replace Vashti¦229388.
\v 18 Then¦229390 the king¦229391 hosted a large¦229393 celebration and invited all his officials¦229396 and servants¦229397 to a feast to celebrate Esther¦229400 \add becoming queen\add*. He proclaimed that this would be a time to celebrate for people in all of the provinces¦229402 of his empire when they wouldn't have to pay taxes, and he gave¦229404 \add many\add* generous gifts.
\s1 Mordekai and Esther earn the king's favour
\rem /s1 Mordecai Discovers a Plot; Mordecai and Haman; Mordecai Saves the King's Life; Mordecai Uncovers a Conspiracy
\p
\v 19 \add Later on,\add* King¦229416 Ahasuerus had \add his officers\add* bring more virgins¦229410 \add to Shushan\add*. During this time, Mordekai¦229412 \add had been given a position by the king so\add* he sat¦229413 \add there\add* at¦229409 the king's¦229416 gate¦229414.
\v 20 Esther¦229419 had still not told¦229426 anyone that \add she was Jewish\add* because Mordekai had warned her¦229421 not to tell anyone. \add What's more,\add* she continued to follow all of¦229431 Mordekai's¦229428 instructions¦229426, just¦229435 as¦229435 she had done¦229434 when she was still under his guardianship¦229437.
\rem /s1 Mordekai's Loyalty to the King
\p
\v 21 One day¦229441 when Mordekai¦229443 was¦229444 \add on duty\add* at¦229445 the king's gate¦229445, two¦229451 of¦229445 the king's guards¦229455 who protected the doorway \add to the king's private rooms\add* became angry¦229448 with¦229445 the king and so they¦229457 planned to assassinate King¦229447 Ahasuerus¦229461. (Their names were Bigthan¦229449 and Teresh¦229450.)
\v 22 But¦229463 when Mordekai heard what they were planning, he told Queen¦229468 Esther¦229467 about¦229472 it and¦229469 she told the king¦229471, explaining that Mordekai had given her the information.
\v 23 When¦229475 the matter¦229485 was investigated¦229475 and \add the facts\add* discovered, both conspirators were impaled on¦229484 \add sharpened wooden\add* poles, and in the presence of the king¦229488, the incident was recorded in the royal record of events.
\c 3
\s1 Haman plans to annihilate the Jews
\rem /s1 Haman's Plot against the Jews; Haman Plots to Destroy the Jews; Haman Undertakes to Destroy the Jews; Haman's Plot to Destroy the Jews
\p
\v 1 Some time later, King¦229496 Ahasuerus¦229497 promoted Haman, one of his officials (and the son¦229501 of Hammedatha¦229503 \add who was a descendant\add* of Agag). The king¦229496 gave Haman a very important position¦229509—more important than any of his other officials.
\v 2 Then¦229517 the king commanded¦229531 that all his other officials had¦229531 to bow down in¦229523 front of Haman to honour him¦229533, and¦229517 they were doing this at¦229523 the king's¦229534 gate¦229523, except for Mordekai¦229535 who refused to bow down to reverence Haman.
\v 3 “Why¦229552 are you¦229550 disobeying the king's command¦229553?” asked the other royal servants¦229542 at¦229546 the king's gate¦229546.
\v 4 Mordekai told them that it was because¦229571 he was a Jew¦229578 \add (and Jews only worship Yahweh)\add*. He¦229563 didn't¦229562 listen¦229563 to their \add warnings\add* so they¦229565 told Haman¦229566 about¦229569 it to see¦229567 if¦229571 he would tolerate¦229568 Mordekai in¦229557 keeping refusing to bow down.
\v 5 When¦229580 Haman¦229581 saw¦229580 that¦229582 Mordekai¦229585 would not¦229584 bow¦229587 down¦229587 to¦229588 him or show him reverence, he¦229589 was¦229589 furious.
\v 6 But¦229593 Haman despised¦229593 the idea of just punishing Mordekai because he'd been told¦229602 that Mordekai \add didn't bow to him because\add* Mordekai was a Jew. So¦229593 Haman¦229609 decided to annihilate¦229610 all of Mordekai's¦229598 people, i.e., all the Jews throughout the kingdom¦229619 of Ahasuerus¦229620.
\p
\v 7 In early April of the twelfth year¦229630 of King¦229633 Ahasuerus' reign, Haman¦229640 got his servants to throw¦229635 dice \add to select the best month and the best day to kill the Jews\add*, and the 13th of March was chosen.
\p
\v 8 Then Haman¦229656 \add went to\add* King¦229657 Ahasuerus¦229658 and said¦229655, “\add Your Majesty,\add* there¦229659 is a certain group of people¦229660 who live among¦229665 the other peoples¦229666 in every province¦229668 of your empire. They have their¦229670 own set of laws, so they¦229679 don't¦229682 obey your laws. It's not¦229679 good for¦229681 you to allow them¦229685 to continue to live in your empire.
\v 9 Your majesty, if you approve of this plan, then write¦229693 a decree saying that all of the¦229703 Jews must¦229700 be destroyed¦229694. \add When they're dead, we can take all of their possessions, and from that\add* I¦229700 will¦229700 give 300 tons of silver¦229699 to your administrators for them to put into¦229707 your royal treasuries¦229709.”
\p
\v 10 \add This seemed sensible to\add* the king¦229713 so he removed¦229712 his signet¦229716 ring from¦229717 his finger and¦229712 he gave¦229719 it¦229719\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Having the king's signet ring enabled Haman to stamp the king's seal (like a signature) onto the newly drafted laws.\f* to Haman¦229720 (the son¦229721 of Hammedatha¦229723, the Agagite¦229724, the adversary of the Jews).
\v 11 The king¦229729 told Haman¦229730, “You can keep the money¦229731 for yourself, and¦229728 you can do whatever you want to do with¦229738 those people¦229734.”
\p
\v 12 On the thirteenth day¦229747 of that same month¦229743, Haman called in the royal scribes, and he dictated a letter to them. He¦229754 told¦229754 them to send copies to the royal officials¦229771, the governors¦229762 of each province, and the leaders of each people group \add within the empire\add*. The scribes translated the letter so it could be sent to each province using its own alphabet and to each people group in its own language¦229779. To show that he was sending the letter under the king's own authority, Haman sealed¦229784 each copy of the letter with¦229743 the ring that had the king's official¦229771 seal on¦229743 it.
\v 13 Couriers delivered the letters to the officials in every province¦229796 in the empire. The letters said to completely destroy¦229798 all the Jews, including¦229800 the children and¦229788 women¦229811, on¦229790 a single¦229813 day. That was to be the thirteenth day of¦229816 March early in the following year. The letters also stated that those who killed¦229799 the Jews could take¦229825 everything that belonged to them.
\v 14 The¦229827 letter told the officials to display¦229835 copies where everyone¦229831 could see them—that way all the people¦229838 in every¦229836 single province¦229833 would know that the king had commanded this¦229842, and¦229834 they could get ready¦229840 for¦229841 when the day¦229841 came.
\p
\v 15 So couriers¦229844 hurried¦229846 off with¦229847 the letters as the king had commanded¦229847. A herald in the capital city¦229857 of Shushan also proclaimed what the letters said. Then the king and Haman¦229854 relaxed and drank¦229856 together, but¦229849 there was¦229859 \add total\add* confusion¦229859 in Shushan \add about how all of this would work out\add*.
\c 4
\s1 Mordekai asks the queen for help
\rem /s1 Mordecai Asks for Esther's Help; Esther Agrees to Help the Jews; Esther plans to rescue the Jews; Mordecai Requests Esther's Help; Mordecai Persuades Esther to Help
\p
\v 1 When¦229871 Mordekai¦229862 found out about¦229874 what had¦229869 been¦229869 done¦229869, he¦229870 tore¦229870 his¦229874 clothes¦229874 and dressed in sackcloth¦229876 and \add threw\add* ashes¦229877 \add over himself (as a sign of mourning or distress)\add* and went¦229878 out into the city¦229880 centre, wailing loudly¦229883 and bitterly¦229884.
\v 2 But¦229886 no one was wearing mourning garb was allowed inside the king's gate, so when Mordekai reached the gate, he had to stay just outside it.
\v 3 In every province¦229904 of the empire, the letter that said to destroy the Jews was publicly displayed and when the Jews heard about¦229906 it¦229921, they were incredibly upset. They skipped meals and wailed¦229918 loudly, and many of them also wore sackcloth¦229919 and threw ashes¦229920 on themselves and lay on the ground.
\p
\v 4 When¦229924 \add Queen\add* Esther's¦229929 young female attendants came¦229924 with her guardians and¦229924 told¦229931 her \add what was going on outside\add*, even the queen¦229934 became very scared. She¦229936 sent¦229936 Mordekai¦229941 some good clothes¦229938 to wear instead of¦229943 the sackcloth¦229943, but¦229924 he¦229946 wouldn't accept¦229946 them¦229946.
\v 5 The king¦229952 had¦229954 assigned some of the royal guardians to Esther personally, so Esther called¦229948 for one of them—a man named¦229948 Hathak¦229950. She¦229956 told¦229956 him¦229956 to go out and speak with Mordekai¦229959 and find out what¦229966 he was¦229968 so distressed about¦229964.
\v 6 So¦229970 Hathak¦229971 went¦229970 out to \add talk to\add* Mordekai¦229974 who was out in the plaza in front¦229980 of the king's¦229983 gate¦229981.
\v 7 Mordekai¦229988 told him everything that¦229998 Haman was planning to do and¦229985 even told him how much money¦229997 Haman had¦229999 said¦229999 the king would get for his treasuries¦230004 if \add the king commanded people\add* to kill¦230009 all the Jews.
\v 8 Mordekai also gave¦230022 Hathak a copy¦230013 of the letter that the announcers had¦230019 read out loud in¦230020 Shushan¦230020, that said that people must kill all the Jews. He told Hathak to show¦230024 the letter to Esther¦230027 so that she would know exactly what it said and also told him to urge her to go¦230032 to the king¦230035 personally and to beg him desperately to save her people \add from destruction\add*.
\v 9 So¦230045 Hathak¦230046 returned to¦230048 the¦230050 queen and¦230047 told¦230047 her what Mordekai¦230051 had said,
\v 10 and¦230056 Esther¦230054 told¦230056 him¦230056 to go back to Mordekai¦230059 with this message:
\v 11 “There's a law about¦230061 going to the king that applies to everyone¦230071 in the kingdom, both men and women¦230074. If anyone¦230089 goes¦230076 into the inner¦230084 courtyard¦230083 of¦230065 the palace, where the king can see them, and the king has¦230105 not¦230086 summoned them, that person¦230065 will be executed¦230091. Only if the king holds¦230094 out his golden¦230101 scepter¦230100 to them, then they will live¦230102. (Everyone¦230089 in the whole empire knows¦230069 this law.) The king hasn't called for me in over a month\add , and if I go in without being summoned, I could be put to death\add*.”
\p
\v 12 \add So Hathak\add* went back to¦230118 Mordekai¦230118 and¦230117 told¦230117 him what the¦230120 queen had said.
\v 13 Mordekai¦230125 told \add Hathak\add* to tell this to Esther¦230129: “Don't¦230130 imagine¦230132 that just because you live there in¦230133 the king's¦230137 palace that you'll be safe when they kill all¦230138 the other Jews.
\v 14 If¦230143 you say nothing at¦230147 all now, someone from some other place¦230153 will rescue the¦230148 Jews, but¦230150 you and¦230156 your relatives will not survive. Who¦230160 knows¦230161, perhaps it¦230151 was for¦230164 a time¦230164 just like¦230165 this¦230165 that¦230148 \add God allowed\add* you to¦230167 become queen¦230167?”
\p
\v 15 Then¦230169 Esther¦230170 sent this reply¦230171 to¦230172 Mordekai¦230174:
\v 16 “Go¦230176 and assemble all¦230180 the Jews who¦230183 live here in Shushan¦230184, and tell them to fast and pray for my sake—tell them to not eat¦230189 or drink¦230192 anything for three days and three nights¦230195. My female attendants and I will also fast in the same way. At the end of the three days, I will go¦230204 in to talk to the king¦230207 even though it's against the law¦230211. I will do that¦230208 even if it costs me my life.”
\p
\v 17 So¦230216 Mordekai¦230217 went¦230216 and¦230218 did everything¦230219 that¦230220 Esther¦230224 had¦230222 told¦230222 him¦230223 to¦230219 do.
\c 5
\s1 Esther goes to the king without an invitation
\rem /s1 Esther's Banquet; Esther Invites the King and Haman to a Banquet; Esther's Request to the King
\p
\v 1 Three days¦230229 later, \add Queen\add* Esther¦230232 put¦230231 on her royal robes¦230231 and \add went and\add* stood¦230234 in the inner¦230239 courtyard¦230235 of¦230235 the palace, across from the king's¦230238 house¦230241. He was¦230244 in the royal palace, sitting¦230244 on the royal throne¦230247 and facing the room's entrance¦230252.
\v 2 As soon as the king¦230257 noticed¦230256 Esther standing¦230262 there in¦230263 the courtyard¦230263, he was very pleased to see¦230256 her. So¦230255 he held¦230267 out his golden¦230273 scepter to her and Esther came up \add to the throne\add* and touched¦230278 the top¦230279 of¦230279 the scepter.
\v 3 “Why have you come here, Queen¦230290 Esther¦230289?” the king¦230285 asked. “What¦230286 do you want? Tell me, and¦230291 I'll give¦230298 you anything you ask for—even half¦230296 of the empire.”
\p
\v 4 “If¦230303 it¦230319 pleases your majesty,” Esther¦230302 replied¦230301, “please come¦230309 with Haman¦230311 today to¦230313 the¦230315 banquet¦230315 that¦230316 I've¦230318 prepared for you.”
\p
\v 5 The¦230322 king said¦230321 to his servants, “\add Go and\add* get Haman” the king told his servants, “and bring¦230323 him quickly¦230323 so that we can do¦230327 what the queen has asked us.” So¦230321 the king and Haman went¦230332 to the banquet¦230337 that Esther¦230331 had had prepared for them.
\v 6 While they were drinking¦230346 wine¦230347, the¦230344 king¦230344 said¦230343 to¦230345 Esther¦230345, “Now please tell me what you \add really\add* want. I'll give¦230351 you anything you ask for, no matter how much it is. I truly mean what I am saying¦230343.”
\p
\v 7 “This is what I \add really\add* want.” Esther¦230363 replied¦230364.
\v 8 “Your majesty, if you're pleased with me and if you're happy to give me what I want, please come¦230388 with Haman¦230390 to \add another\add* banquet¦230393 that¦230394 I'll prepare for¦230396 you tomorrow¦230397 and I'll answer your question then.”
\s1 Haman's anger with Mordekai and his execution plan
\rem /s1 Haman Plots to Kill Mordecai; Haman's Plan to Kill Mordecai; Haman Plans to Have Mordecai Hanged; Haman's Rage Against Mordecai
\p
\v 9 Haman was feeling very happy as he left \add the banquet\add* that day¦230404. But¦230402 then¦230402 he saw¦230409 Mordekai sitting at¦230414 the king's¦230415 gate¦230414 and¦230402 noticed¦230409 that Mordekai didn't¦230416 stand¦230418 up¦230418 or show fearful reverence to him¦230422. This made Haman furious with¦230414 Mordekai
\v 10 but¦230430 he restrained¦230430 himself¦230430 and went¦230432 home¦230435. Then¦230430 he sent¦230436 for his friends¦230440 and brought¦230437 in his wife¦230444, Zeresh¦230443,
\v 11 boasting to¦230447 them¦230447 about how rich he was and about how many sons¦230454 he had, and how the king had promoted¦230463 him \add many times\add* and given him a position above¦230464 all of¦230467 the other officials¦230466 and administrators.
\v 12 “And that is not¦230473 all,” Haman¦230471 added. “I¦230494 was the only one that Queen¦230477 Esther¦230476 invited to go with the king to a banquet¦230483 that she prepared¦230486 for us today. And she's also¦230491 invited only me to go with the king again tomorrow¦230493.
\v 13 However, I still can't¦230505 be happy as long as I keep seeing¦230513 that¦230504 Jew¦230517, Mordekai¦230516, sitting¦230518 there at¦230519 the king's¦230520 gate¦230519.”
\p
\v 14 Then Zeresh¦230524 his wife¦230525 and all¦230526 his friends¦230528 said¦230522 to him, “Get them to install 25-metre high¦230532 pole, and in¦230535 the morning¦230535 get permission from the king, and get them to impale Mordekai¦230542 on¦230535 it. Then you can go¦230544 with¦230535 the king to the banquet¦230553 and enjoy yourself.”
\p That idea sounded good¦230555 to Haman¦230558, so he¦230559 got the¦230560 pole erected.
\c 6
\s1 The king honours Mordekai
\rem /s1 Haman executed; The King Honours Mordecai; Mordecai Honoured
\p
\v 1 That¦230570 night¦230563 the king¦230567 was unable to sleep¦230566 so he ordered the royal records¦230573 to be fetched and to be read¦230577 to him.
\v 2 The¦230591 records¦230582 mentioned that Bigthan¦230588 and¦230581 Teresh¦230589, two¦230590 of¦230591 the royal guardians¦230593 who protected the doorway to the king's¦230592 private quarters, had planned to assassinate the king, but¦230581 that Mordekai¦230585 had reported it¦230581.
\v 3 “What honour¦230607 or recognition did I give Mordekai¦230609 for saving my life?” the king asked.
\p “Nothing was done for him,” replied¦230602 the young¦230614 men¦230614 who were attending¦230616 him.
\p
\v 4 At that moment, Haman¦230627 had entered the outer¦230633 courtyard of the king's¦230624 house¦230630. He had come¦230628 to tell the king that he wanted to execute Mordekai¦230639 on¦230626 the pole that he'd had set up for him¦230646.
\p “Who's out in¦230626 the courtyard?” the king¦230632 asked.
\p
\v 5 “Haman's¦230653 out there¦230652 standing¦230654 in¦230655 the courtyard¦230655,” replied¦230648 the young men.
\p “Bring¦230652 him¦230658 in then¦230648,” the¦230649 king¦230657 instructed.
\p
\v 6 When¦230660 Haman¦230661 came¦230660 in¦230668, the king¦230664 asked him, “What¦230665 should I do for a man¦230668 that I would really like to honour¦230672?”
\p Haman thought to himself, “Surely it must¦230677 be me that the king would like to honour more¦230682 than¦230682 anyone else?”
\v 7 so he¦230684 replied¦230684 to the king, “If¦230685 you really want to honour¦230693 someone,
\v 8 tell your servants to bring one of¦230696 your own royal robes that you have already worn¦230699 yourself and also bring a horse¦230703 that you have already ridden¦230705 yourself and put¦230709 a royal crown on¦230712 its¦230712 head¦230712.
\v 9 Then¦230714 have one of your most noble¦230724 officials¦230722 present the man with¦230732 the robe and the horse. Have your servants put¦230714 the robe on the man whom you really want to honour and have them seat that man on the horse and then¦230714 lead the horse through the public square¦230737 of the city¦230738 while they¦230739 shout out \add to everyone\add* in¦230732 front¦230740 of them, ‘The¦230745 king¦230745 is doing¦230742 this because he¦230746 really wants to honour this man!’ ”
\p
\v 10 The¦230750 king¦230750 \add agreed so\add* he¦230749 replied¦230749 to Haman¦230751, “Go quickly and take¦230753 the robe and the horse¦230759 and do what you've just described for¦230765 Mordekai¦230765 the Jew¦230766 who¦230767 sits¦230767 by the gate¦230768 to the palace. Make¦230772 sure that you do absolutely everything that you've said¦230776.”
\p
\v 11 So¦230778 Haman¦230779 got the robe and the horse and put the robe on Mordekai¦230789, seated him on the horse, and then¦230778 led the horse through the public square¦230791 of the city¦230792. As he did, he shouted out to everyone in¦230791 front¦230794 of him, “The¦230799 king¦230799 is doing this because he really wants to honour¦230801 this man¦230797!”
\p
\v 12 Afterwards, Mordekai¦230804 returned¦230803 to the king's¦230808 gate¦230807 but¦230803 Haman¦230809 hurried¦230810 to his house¦230813, covering¦230815 his head¦230816 like a mourner¦230814.
\v 13 \add At home\add* he explained to Zeresh¦230820 his wife and to all¦230822 his friends¦230824 everything that¦230828 had¦230829 happened¦230829 to him. Then¦230818 his advisors¦230833 and his wife said¦230830 to him, “Mordekai¦230839 has begun¦230841 to defeat you and since he is¦230837 one¦230837 of the Jewish¦230838 people¦230837, you won't¦230844 win against him—instead, he'll certainly¦230850 defeat you.”
\s1 Haman's execution
\rem /s1 Haman Is Put to Death; Haman's Downfall and Mordecai's Advancement
\p
\v 14 While they¦230854 were¦230855 still talking¦230855 together, \add some of\add* the royal guardians arrived¦230859 to bring¦230861 Haman¦230864 quickly to the banquet¦230867 that¦230868 Esther¦230871 had had prepared¦230870.
\c 7
\rem /s1 The King Executes Haman; Haman Hanged
\p
\v 1 So¦230873 the¦230874 king¦230874 and¦230875 Haman¦230875 went¦230873 to¦230876 Queen¦230880 Esther's¦230879 \add banquet\add*.
\v 2 While they were drinking¦230888 wine¦230889 at¦230888 that second¦230887 banquet, the king¦230883 asked Esther again, “Now please tell me what you really want, Queen¦230894 Esther. Tell me, and¦230882 I'll do¦230904 it for you—I'll give¦230895 you anything you ask for, no matter what it is.”
\p
\v 3 “Your¦230914 majesty,” Queen¦230908 Esther¦230907 replied¦230909, “if¦230916 you are pleased with¦230914 me, I hope you'll be¦230922 willing to do what I ask. Please allow me to live, and¦230906 please save my people¦230927. That is what I am asking for
\v 4 because someone has turned me and my¦230933 people¦230933 over \add to our enemies\add* and they're going to completely destroy¦230934 us. If¦230937 someone had¦230940 \add just\add* sold¦230931 the men and even the women to be slaves¦230939, I¦230932 wouldn't have said anything, because that wouldn't have been important enough to bother your majesty with¦230946.”
\p
\v 5 “Who¦230956 has done this¦230958?” King¦230951 Ahasuerus¦230952 asked her. “Where¦230959 is¦230962 the man who¦230956 has dared to do such a thing?”
\p
\v 6 “The man who is our bitter enemy¦230975 is this evil¦230977 man Haman¦230976!” answered¦230970 Esther¦230972.
\p \add By this time,\add* Haman was¦230980 terrified¦230980 standing there in front¦230981 of the king¦230982 and¦230970 the queen¦230983.
\v 7 Now the king became so angry that he got up¦230986 and abandoned his wine-drinking at¦230987 the banquet and went outside into¦230990 the palace¦230993 garden¦230992. But¦230985 Haman¦230994 stayed inside to beg his life¦230999 from¦231000 Queen¦231001 Esther¦231000 because¦231002 he recognized that the king wanted to \add execute\add* him.
\v 8 \add As he was pleading for his life,\add* Haman¦231022 \add knelt down\add* very close to Esther¦231028 as she was \add reclining\add* on¦231038 a couch¦231026. When¦231012 the king¦231012 returned¦231013 from the palace¦231015 garden¦231014 to the room where they had been drinking wine¦231021, he \add saw this and\add* exclaimed, “\add Now\add* he's¦231030 even trying to rape the queen¦231036 in my presence and in my own house¦231018!”
\p As soon as the¦231036 king said¦231030 this, some of¦231043 his servants covered¦231045 Haman's¦231044 face¦231043 \add (as a sign that he would be executed)\add*.
\v 9 Then Harbona¦231049, who was one¦231050 of¦231051 the guardians who served the king¦231079 personally, said¦231078, “Your majesty, Haman¦231063 has¦231062 also¦231056 set up a pole twenty-five metres high¦231075 at his house¦231073 because he wants to hang¦231080 Mordekai on it. But¦231048 Mordekai saved your life.”
\p “Execute Haman on it!” the king commanded.
\p
\v 10 So¦231083 they¦231083 impaled Haman¦231086 on¦231087 the pole that¦231090 he¦231092 had¦231092 set up for¦231093 Mordekai¦231093, and¦231083 then¦231083 the king's¦231095 anger¦231094 dissipated.
\c 8
\s1 The Jews are enabled to defend themselves
\rem /s1 Esther Saves the Jews; The King's Edict in Behalf of the Jews; A Decree to Help the Jews; The Jews Are Told to Fight Back
\p
\v 1 That same day¦231099, King¦231102 Ahasuerus¦231103 gave¦231101 Queen Esther all the property that had belonged to Haman¦231109, the enemy of¦231108 the Jews. Esther told¦231119 the king that Mordekai was \add her cousin and that he had been like a father\add* to her, and \add so the king summoned\add* Mordekai to come¦231114 to him.
\v 2 Then¦231126 the king¦231127 removed¦231126 his¦231130 signet¦231130 ring which¦231131 he¦231132 had retrieved from¦231133 Haman, and¦231126 gave¦231134 it¦231134 to Mordekai. The¦231143 queen also put Mordekai in charge of¦231143 all the property that¦231131 had belonged to Haman.
\rem /s1 The triumphant Jews destroy their enemies
\p
\v 3 Then Esther¦231148 came to speak¦231149 to the king¦231151 again¦231147, and she knelt down and put her face¦231153 right on top of his¦231154 feet¦231154. She¦231155 cried as she begged him to stop Haman's¦231163 terrible plan¦231166 to destroy the Jews.
\v 4 The¦231180 king held¦231173 out his golden¦231178 scepter¦231177 toward Esther¦231175, so she¦231181 stood¦231181 up and¦231173 waited facing the king.
\v 5 “Your majesty,” she¦231185 said¦231185, “if you think that it's¦231197 the right¦231197 thing to do, and¦231192 if you're pleased with¦231203 me, please write a new letter¦231208 revoking¦231205 the letters¦231208 that Haman¦231210 (the son¦231211 of Hammedatha¦231213, the Agagite¦231214) sent out \add instructing the people\add* to destroy¦231217 all¦231222 the Jews throughout your empire.
\v 6 I couldn't bear to see the terrible thing that¦231232 is about¦231237 to happen to my people¦231237. I \add certainly\add* couldn't bear to see \add all\add* my relatives destroyed¦231241!”
\p
\v 7 Then king¦231246 Ahasuerus¦231247 said¦231245 to Queen¦231249 Esther and to Mordekai¦231250 the Jew¦231251, “Listen, I've¦231256 given¦231256 the house¦231253 of¦231253 Haman¦231255 to Esther, and they've¦231259 executed him¦231258 on¦231268 the pole because he¦231266 attempted to destroy the Jews.
\v 8 But¦231271 as you \add both\add* know, no one can revoke¦231296 a letter that¦231286 has my name and my official seal on it. So¦231271 this is what you should do: write¦231272 \add a new letter\add* to help the Jews¦231275, as you think best and put my name on it and seal the letter with¦231277 my signet ring.”
\p
\v 9 It was now June, and the king¦231301 sent for his scribes and they¦231298 wrote a letter to the Jews containing everything that¦231330 Mordekai¦231320 dictated. It was also addressed to royal officials¦231328, and to the governors¦231327 and leaders in each province. The empire had 127 provinces¦231340, extending all the way from India¦231332 \add in the east\add* to Ethiopia¦231335 \add in the west\add*. The scribes wrote to every province using its own alphabet and to each people group in its own language, and to the Jews in their language and using their alphabet.
\v 10 The letters were written¦231352 in¦231357 King¦231354 Ahasuerus' name¦231353, and¦231352 \add each one\add* was sealed¦231356 with¦231357 the king's signet¦231357 ring. Then¦231352 the letters were distributed by¦231361 mounted¦231363 couriers¦231362 riding¦231364 on¦231357 swift horses¦231365 bred from the royal¦231366 mares¦231368.
\p
\v 11 The letters stated that¦231396 the king¦231372 permitted¦231371 the Jews in¦231376 each¦231376 and every¦231391 city¦231380 to get together and defend¦231382 themselves, and what's more to destroy¦231386, kill¦231386, and annihilate¦231388 all the forces of any people¦231394 or province¦231395 hostile to them¦231397, including¦231380 women¦231399 and children, and \add then\add* to plunder¦231401 their possessions.
\v 12 \add This was to take place\add* on a certain day in¦231410 every province¦231407 throughout the empire: the 13th of March\x + \xo 8:12: \xt 3:7.\x* \add (the same day on which Haman's law was to take effect)\add*.
\v 13 A copy¦231421 of¦231421 the decree was¦231423 to be included as law¦231424 in¦231425 every¦231430 province¦231427 and¦231433 to be displayed¦231429 to all the people¦231432 so that the Jews could be prepared to fight back against their¦231441 enemies¦231441 on that day¦231438.
\v 14 So the couriers¦231443 on¦231450 the royal¦231446 horses¦231445 rode¦231444 out¦231447 quickly under the king's¦231451 orders to deliver the letters. The¦231452 new law¦231452 was¦231453 also proclaimed throughout the capital city of Shushan¦231454.
\p
\v 15 The king¦231462 \add gave\add* Mordekai royal¦231464 clothing¦231463 that was blue and white¦231466, with¦231463 a large¦231469 gold¦231468 crown¦231467, and a purple¦231472 robe¦231470 made from fine¦231471 linen¦231471. The people of Shushan¦231474 cheered happily when Mordekai exited \add the palace\add*.
\v 16 For the Jews, it¦231479 was \add a time\add* of light¦231480 and happiness, and they were honoured¦231483 \add by those around them\add*.
\v 17 In every single province¦231487 and in every single city, wherever¦231494 the letter announcing the king's¦231497 decree was brought, the Jews were very cheerful¦231501 and had¦231512 big celebrations. Many¦231506 people¦231507 from other groups in the empire became¦231509 afraid of the Jews, so they \add converted to Judaism\add* themselves.
\c 9
\s1 The Jews destroy their enemies
\rem /s1 The Jews Destroy Their Enemies; Triumph of the Jews; The Victory of the Jews; Destruction of the Enemies of the Jews
\p
\v 1 \add Finally\add* when the 13th of March arrived, it was time for everyone to do what the letters from the king¦231533 said that he had decreed for them to do. The enemies of the Jews had expected to destroy the Jews on¦231518 that day but¦231518 just the opposite happened—instead, it was the Jews who¦231549 destroyed their¦231549 enemies.
\v 2 Throughout the empire, the Jews joined together in their¦231571 cities¦231553 to defend themselves against those¦231561 who¦231561 wanted to harm¦231562 them. No one was able to fight back against them because¦231568 everyone¦231563 \add else\add* in the empire had¦231570 become afraid of them.
\v 3 All¦231578 the leaders in each¦231578 province¦231581, the royal officials¦231580, the governors¦231583, and¦231578 everyone¦231578 who worked¦231585 for¦231587 the king¦231587 helped the Jews¦231591 because¦231592 they had¦231594 become afraid of Mordekai¦231597
\v 4 because¦231611 he was¦231602 \add now\add* a very important royal official, and his¦231606 fame spread throughout the provinces¦231610 as he became¦231616 more and more powerful¦231616.
\v 5 So the¦231620 Jews took their weapons and fought against all¦231621 of¦231623 their enemies and completely destroyed¦231628 them. They were able to do everything that they¦231631 wanted to do against their enemies.
\p
\v 6 In¦231633 the capital city of Shushan, the Jews killed¦231635 500 men¦231640
\v 7 including¦231645 \add Haman's ten sons\add* Parshandatha¦231644, Dalphon¦231647, Aspatha¦231650,
\v 8 Poratha¦231654, Adalia¦231657, Aridatha¦231660,
\v 9 Parmashta¦231664, Arisai¦231667, Aridai¦231670, and¦231662 Vaizatha¦231673.
\v 10 Those were the ten¦231675 sons¦231676 of Haman¦231677 (son¦231678 of Hammedatha¦231680), the enemy of the Jews, but¦231684 the Jews didn't¦231685 take their¦231689 possessions.
\p
\v 11 At the end of the day¦231691, the king¦231699 received a report about¦231694 how many people the Jews had killed¦231695 in¦231696 the capital city of Shushan¦231696
\v 12 and he¦231702 told Queen¦231705 Esther¦231704, “Here in¦231706 the capital city of Shushan¦231706 the Jews have killed 500 men, including¦231702 Haman's¦231718 ten¦231715 sons¦231716. In¦231719 the rest¦231719 of my empire, they must¦231727 have killed many more¦231732 than that! So tell me what¦231724 else you want and I'll do it¦231727 for¦231728 you. I'll do whatever you ask, so do tell me.”
\p
\v 13 “If¦231737 it¦231743 seems like a good¦231742 plan to you, your majesty,” Esther¦231736 replied¦231735, “then¦231735 please allow the Jews¦231747 who¦231748 live here in¦231749 Shushan¦231749 to do again tomorrow¦231746 what you allowed them to do today. Also¦231735, command that the bodies of Haman's¦231757 ten¦231754 sons¦231755 be impaled on¦231749 the wooden pole.”
\v 14 Then¦231763 the king¦231764 ordered for this to¦231765 be¦231765 done¦231765 and a decree was¦231767 made throughout in¦231769 Shushan¦231769, and they \add publicly\add* impaled \add the bodies of\add*Haman's¦231774 ten¦231771 sons¦231772.
\v 15 So¦231777 the next day¦231784 on¦231782 the 14th, the Jews in Shushan gathered¦231777 together again and¦231777 killed¦231789 300 \add more\add* men in Shushan. But¦231777 \add once again\add* they¦231796 did not take the things that belonged to those men.
\p
\v 16 The Jews in¦231804 the other parts of the empire, who had gathered together to fight for their lives¦231811 had defeated their enemies and¦231801 killed¦231814 75,000 of them. But¦231801 they \add too\add* did not take the things that had belonged to their enemies.
\v 17 That¦231831 all happened on the 13th \add (as the law had said)\add*, and then they¦231832 stopped on the 14th and made¦231836 it¦231837 a day¦231838 of¦231838 feasting¦231839 and celebration.
\rem /s1 Purim Celebrated; The Feast of Purim Inaugurated
\v 18 But¦231842 the Jews in¦231846 Shushan¦231846 had gathered together \add and fought their enemies\add* on both the 13th and the 14th, so they¦231854 stopped on the 15th and made¦231858 it¦231859 a day¦231860 of¦231860 feasting¦231861 and celebration.
\v 19 So¦231864 that's why the Jews who live¦231870 in¦231871 the rural¦231872 villages observe¦231873 this holiday on¦231871 the 14th rather than the 15th. They celebrate with¦231871 feasting¦231881 and by giving gifts to each other.
\s1 The celebration named ‘Purim’
\rem /s1 The Festival of Purim; The feast of Purim
\p
\v 20 Now Mordekai¦231891 recorded¦231890 everything that had happened and sent¦231896 letters¦231897 to¦231898 all the Jews throughout the empire, in both the near¦231909 provinces¦231906 and the far¦231910 away ones.
\v 21 That decree established an annual holiday on¦231913 the¦231920 14th and¦231922 15th of¦231923 March every year,\f + \fr 9:21 \ft The OET-RV gives an approximate time of the year in our calendars, but the Jewish calendar is largely a lunar calendar so the dates of the feast in our calendars moves around a little. In 2024 for example, ‘Purim’ begins on the evening of March 23 in Yisrael/Israel and concludes at sundown on March 24.\f*
\v 22 because those were the days when the Jews no longer \add had to oppose\add* their enemies¦231939 and that was the month¦231940 when their sorrow¦231944 and mourning¦231946 had changed¦231942 into¦231947 a good¦231948 day¦231947. Now they¦231936 were instructed to make them¦231950 days of feasting¦231952 and happiness, and sending¦231954 of gifts¦231958—to each other but¦231940 also to the needy.
\v 23 The¦231962 Jews had already celebrated those days that¦231964 way, so they¦231966 \add readily\add* agreed to do¦231967 what Mordekai¦231972 had written¦231971.
\p
\v 24 Yes, Haman¦231976 (the son¦231977 of Hammedatha¦231979, the Agagite¦231980), the enemy of all¦231982 the Jews, had plotted to annihilate them, and he¦231990 had thrown¦231990 a ‘Pur¦231991’ (which is¦231993 ‘lot¦231993’ or perhaps ‘dice’) \add choose the date\add* to crush¦231994 them and to destroy¦231995 them.
\v 25 But¦231997 \add instead they would remember how Queen Esther stood\add* in front¦231998 of¦231998 the king¦231999, and how he had¦232009 said¦232000 in the letter¦232003, “Let¦232004 his evil¦232006 plot that¦232007 he plotted concerning the Jews return¦232004 on his head¦232015, and let¦232004 them execute him¦232017 and his sons¦232020 on the pole.”
\v 26 Therefore¦232025 they called¦232028 this celebration ‘Purim¦232031’\f + \fr 9:26 \ft Plural of Hebrew ‘Pur’ meaning ‘lot/dice’.\f* because of what¦232046 they had experienced and because of the contents of \add Mordekai's\add* letter¦232044.
\v 27 The Jews agreed to establish those two¦232077 days as holidays and to observe¦232075 them on those specific days as they'd been instructed. They agreed that they and their¦232066 descendants¦232066 and everyone¦232068 who became part of the Jewish people would celebrate it¦232073 every year.
\v 28 So¦232087 that's why every Jewish family¦232096 in every generation¦232093 \add since\add* has celebrated these days as holidays, wherever they¦232105 live. The Jewish community and¦232087 its descendants¦232112 will always faithfully observe¦232090 this festival of ‘Purim¦232102’.
\p
\v 29 Then Queen¦232117 Esther¦232116 (the daughter¦232118 of¦232118 Abihail¦232120), with¦232123 \add help from\add* Mordekai¦232121 the Jew¦232122, wrote¦232115 a second¦232133 letter¦232130 about¦232118 ‘Purim¦232131’.
\v 30 He¦232135 sent¦232135 this \add second\add* letter¦232136 to¦232137 all¦232139 the Jews throughout the empire of King Ahasuerus¦232149, with words¦232150 of peace¦232151 and¦232145 truth¦232152
\v 31 to set up the ‘Purim¦232158’ celebration on¦232160 the given dates according to what Mordekai¦232164 the Jew¦232165 and Queen¦232167 Esther¦232166 had determined for them¦232163. \add They also confirmed\add* that the Jews should continue the times¦232160 of fasting¦232177 and mourning that they¦232169 had established¦232154 for themselves and their¦232172 future descendants¦232175.
\v 32 So¦232180 Esther's¦232181 decree confirmed¦232182 these¦232185 regulations about¦232180 ‘Purim¦232184’ and¦232180 they were written¦232186 into the \add official\add* record.
\c 10
\s1 The grandeur of King Ahasuerus and Mordekai
\rem /s1 The Greatness of Mordecai; The Greatness of Xerxes and Mordecai
\p
\v 1 Then King¦232191 Ahasuerus¦232192 imposed a tax¦232195 \add on everyone\add* throughout his empire, including¦232190 \add those\add* on the islands¦232199 of¦232199 the \add Mediterranean\add* Sea¦232200.
